Changes: standalone character-conversion API, factorization without
nontriviality restrictions, explicit exceptional values, and norm preservation.
The Gauss norm theorem is reused from this project's ArithmeticEffectivityTheory.

The primary-prime proof was read from the Kontorovich mirror, commit
d26d846d6f3129017f06b6fd3f78dd72d361d60b. The local adaptation derives the
unit multiplier from equality of principal ideals, exposes the canonical
primary generator, and gives the normalized identity for every primitive
additive character.

The composite Gauss-sum module factors arbitrary primitive additive characters
through the actual CRT isomorphism. Its prime-product Möbius proof is adapted
from SecondPoissonScale; its character factorization and construction are local.

The Hasse–Davenport proof in MobiusBecomesACubicGaussCoefficient is adapted
from the JacobiQuadratic namespace in CubicNormalization. Its actual-prime
and CRT endpoints, explicit conversion factor, and modulus proof use the
local primary-generator and product-character APIs.

CubicReciprocityFromFrobenius adapts the Frobenius argument from CubicTrace
and injectivity on cubic roots from QuadraticTrace. The canonical-generator
endpoint uses the local primary-generator construction.

TheCubicPhaseLivesModuloTwo adapts the fixed-two reciprocity specialization
from SquarePhaseFactorization. Its proof of inertness uses ideal norms and
the existing cubic-root cardinality relation, avoiding prime-factor classification.

EisensteinCoordinates adapts the integral coordinate basis and rational
quotient parametrization. Its intrinsic field-trace formulas and embedding
independent norm proof use the local cyclotomic field and conjugation APIs.

BinaryGaussianPoisson extracts the two successive Gaussian transforms,
positive-real-part summability proofs, square-root branch calculation, and
explicit oscillatory inverse form from the listed Poisson sources.

ShiftedEisensteinGaussianMass adapts the affine Gaussian transform and
dominated-convergence Abel limit from BinaryGaussian, QuadraticNormalization,
GaussianDualLimit, and GaussianFiberMass. It uses the local binary transform.

QuadraticTraceCoordinates derives the actual trace pairing and identifies
the dual phase intrinsically through the modulus-four character. Its finite
residue evaluation follows the quadratic phase argument in RamifiedTracePhase.

EisensteinResidueGaussianMass adapts the residue-fibre partition and lattice
Abel limit from GaussianFiberMass and QuadraticNormalization. It uses the
intrinsic trace character and coordinate equivalence proved locally.

TheDualGaussianPhaseFreezes extracts the Gaussian moment domination,
vanishing normalized phase error, and dual-scale limit from GaussianMoments,
OscillatoryGaussian, AbelPhaseLimit, and RationalApproximation.

TheQuadraticGaussPhaseLivesModuloFour assembles the Abel-limit comparison
from ThetaLocalRows. The local proof keeps the varying dual phase on the
integer lattice and reindexes only the fixed phase into actual residue classes.

TheAnalyticConversionHasAFixedRayPhase adapts the finite square-sum
factorization from SquarePhaseFactorization. Its analytic character bridge,
explicit finite ray factor, and angular decomposition use the local trace
normalization and quadratic Gauss evaluation.

CubicReciprocityAtACommonCharacteristic adapts the primary factorization
and auxiliary negative-sum argument from SquarePhaseFactorization. Its
prime-cover bound uses mathlib's ramification-inertia degree identity.

CubicJacobiSymbols adapts ideal-factor symbol definitions and congruence from
CubicJacobi and coprime cubic-root identities from RayCoefficients. Composite
reciprocity uses the local primary element factorization directly; numerator
multiplicativity is proved for every ideal denominator, including zero.

TheRamifiedCubicSupplement adapts the auxiliary-prime comparison and nine
primary coordinate cases from CubicJacobi. The local proof derives residue
cardinalities directly from the intrinsic integral norm and Jacobi identity.

TheRamifiedCubicPhaseLivesModuloNine adapts the multiplicative coordinate
phases and denominator-congruence proof from RamifiedSymbol. It uses the
local composite symbol and prime supplementary laws.

TheCubicKubotaMultiplier adapts the coprime shift and matrix product law
from RayCoefficients and the finite-index kernel construction from
HighKernelGeometry. The explicit primitive-valued matrix, surjectivity onto
the integral cubic roots, and exact relative kernel index are local extensions.

TheCubicMultiplierExtendsOverRationalMatrices adapts rational conjugation,
Euclidean symbol reduction, and character extension from
lean/OAI/NumberTheory/DirichletL/Reciprocity/KubotaCharacter.lean. The local
finite-index normal-kernel endpoint uses the previously constructed actual kernel.

EisensteinNearestPoint adapts nearest-neighbor comparison from
QuadraticSieve/HighKernelGeometry.lean. The local proof obtains the nearest
point from finite coordinate sublevels and does not choose the sign of the
complex embedding.

HyperbolicThreeSpace adapts row energy and quotient height from
Eisenstein/Rows.lean and Eisenstein/HyperbolicSpace.lean; Iwasawa factorization
from QuadraticSieve/HighKernelGeometry.lean; quotient topology and coordinates
from Eisenstein/QuotientTopology.lean, QuadraticSieve/GlobalBinLoss.lean,
and QuadraticSieve/SelectedExponents.lean.

BianchiReductionAndCompactCore adapts integral row bounds from Eisenstein/Rows.lean,
Ford reduction from QuadraticSieve/HighKernelGeometry.lean, and the finite-index
orbit-quotient cover from Eisenstein/QuotientTopology.lean. The nearest-point
input is the embedding-independent theorem proved locally.

TheCubicEisensteinSeries adapts the bottom-row quotient, lattice majorant,
normal convergence, and automorphy from Eisenstein/Rows.lean and
Eisenstein/HyperbolicSpace.lean. It uses the locally constructed cubic
character and hyperbolic quotient, without an assumed analytic continuation.

TheCubicKernelHasAMeasurableFundamentalDomain adapts the Gram embedding from
QuadraticSieve/GlobalBinLoss.lean and the free action and lexicographic
selector from Eisenstein/FundamentalDomain.lean. The local endpoint exposes
pointwise uniqueness and applies to every Borel measure.

HyperbolicVolumeIsInvariant adapts the Euclidean Jacobian argument from
CubicSieve/ArithmeticBins.lean and the Möbius coordinates, Bruhat
factorization, and invariant pushforward from Eisenstein/HyperbolicVolume.lean.

TheCubicQuotientHasFiniteVolume adapts quotient measure and descent from
Eisenstein/FundamentalDomain.lean, the height integral from
QuadraticSieve/CuspCoercivity.lean, and the finite cusp-box cover bound from
QuadraticSieve/SelectedExponents.lean. Its positive finite volume endpoint
uses the actual kernel and invariant measure constructed locally.

TheCubicQuotientHasLocalMeasureCharts adapts Frobenius displacement bounds,
proper discontinuity, covering projections, and local measure transport from
CubicSieve/LiteralSources.lean. The local second-countability result applies
to every integral subgroup quotient.

SmoothCutoffsOnTheCubicQuotient adapts Euclidean coordinates and local measure
transport from MeanSquare/TransformedColumns.lean, GaussSum/ReflectedBranches.lean,
and Eisenstein/LocalCutoffs.lean. The local endpoint constructs a cutoff at
every actual quotient point without assuming a smooth quotient atlas.

